CHAPTER 11 (HOMOMORPHISMS) — SURVEY + PROBES, 2026-10-16. Nothing written to std/ yet.

The chapter is two sections: §11.1 Group Homomorphisms, §11.2 The Isomorphism Theorems
(First, Second, Correspondence, Third), 25 exercises plus an automorphism appendix.

WHAT STD HAS. Nothing of chapter 11 — no homomorphism, no kernel, no image of a general
phi, no factor-group sort for abstract groups, no product set HN. What it has is
REUSABLE PATTERNS, three of them, and they are enough:

  std/group/isomorphism.b4m   the two-declared-groups preamble (sort G/H, their ops and
                              axioms, model GIsAGroup/HIsAGroup). A homomorphism file
                              copies it verbatim and drops `psi` + its two round-trip
                              axioms. Note phiPreservesIdentity there is ALREADY
                              pure-homomorphism content (its proof uses only
                              phiPreservesOp, opGIdentityLeft and cancellation in H).
  std/group/generated.b4m     a DEFINED subgroup predicate with its three closure
                              properties as THEOREMS — the template for
                              inKernel(g) = (phi(g) = EH).
  std/integer/mod-n.b4m       a genuine QUOTIENT SORT: `sort Zn` + `func cls`,
                              clsSurjective, a congruence axiom, and the operations
                              posited as cls-homomorphism equations. The group axioms
                              are then THEOREMS proved by unpack-representatives.

PROBED, AND BOTH FILES CHECK STRICT. Following CHAPTER9-PAINPOINTS' rule — "probe by
writing the file, not by reading the library" — rather than estimating.

  homomorphism-and-kernel.b4m (7 theorems)
    Prop 11.4(1) phiPreservesIdentity, 11.4(2) phiPreservesInverses,
    the kernel as a predicate, theKernelHasTheIdentity, and
    THEOREM 11.6: theKernelIsNormal. All of §11.1's provable content is reachable.

  factor-group-and-first-iso.b4m (8 theorems)
    THE FACTOR GROUP G/N AS A SORT, on mod-n's pattern — which CLOSES the gap
    aata/10.1 recorded as blocking ("the first needs the cosets themselves as a sort
    to carry the operation (there is none)"). opQAssoc proves by unpack-representatives.
    Then the FIRST ISOMORPHISM THEOREM's whole content: etaIsWellDefined (Judson's own
    first step, and the one the quotient sort is needed for — cls(a) = cls(b) is an
    EQUATION, so `rewrite` can use it), etaPreservesOp, etaIsInjective.

THE ONE REAL LIMIT, and it is narrower than it looks. The RELATION `=~` cannot be
NAMED (CHAPTER9-PAINPOINTS): a group here is a theory — a sort plus operations plus
axioms — not a value in a sort of groups, so "for all groups G, H" is out of reach.
Every isomorphism theorem's conclusion is literally of the form X =~ Y. BUT §9.1
already settled how this goes: every INSTANCE is provable, only the quantification is
not. So First/Second/Third are written as "here are the declared groups, here is the
map, and it is a well-defined injective homomorphism onto" — the mathematical content
complete, the one-line `=~` restatement absent. §9.1's Theorem 9.10 is the precedent.

CORRECTION, 2026-10-16 — I PREDICTED THE CORRESPONDENCE THEOREM WAS BLOCKED. It is not.

My scope note below said to "expect this to be the one theorem stated-but-not-
transliterated, like 9.1's =~", on the reasoning that it quantifies over SUBGROUPS of
G/N and a subgroup is a predicate with no sort of predicates to range over. I then
probed it instead of asserting it, and all of it proves (correspondence.b4m, 10
theorems):

  forward       H/N is a subgroup of G/N, for a given H containing N
  backward      the pullback {g : gN in S} is a subgroup of G containing N, for a
                given subgroup S of G/N
  surjectivity  that pullback's image IS S -- extensional agreement of the two
                predicates on Quot, which is what "S = H/N" MEANS when a subgroup is
                a predicate
  injectivity   containingNMakesClassesSufficient: if cls(a) = cls(b), b is in H2 and
                N is inside H2, then a is in H2. Judson's "since N is contained in H2,
                we know that h1 in H2" -- the step I expected to be unreachable.

The quantification over SETS of subgroups is still out of reach, exactly as =~ is. But
that is the same narrow limit §9.1 settled, not a different one: every instance is
provable and only the "one-to-one correspondence between the two SETS" phrasing is not.
So the chapter has ONE limit, appearing in several places, rather than two.

THE ERROR IS THE ONE CHAPTER9-PAINPOINTS ALREADY NAMES: "All three guesses came from
reasoning about what std HAS rather than what the language can EXPRESS." I reasoned
from the absence of a sort of predicates to the unreachability of a theorem whose
content needs no such sort -- and I did it in the same breath as quoting that file's
rule. Probing took twenty minutes.

SCOPE, as measured rather than guessed:
  §11.1                      reachable now; the probe covers it.
  First Isomorphism          reachable now; the probe covers its three hard steps.
  Second Isomorphism         needs the PRODUCT SET HN (does not exist; subgroup.b4m's
                             inSubgroup2 pattern is the template, and normality of the
                             second subgroup is what makes HN closed) and H n N, which
                             DOES exist as the conjunction, with its three closure
                             theorems already proved in subgroup.b4m.
  Correspondence             quantifies over SUBGROUPS of G/N — "H |-> H/N is a
                             one-to-one correspondence between sets of subgroups". That
                             is the =~ problem one level up: a subgroup is a predicate,
                             and there is no sort of predicates. Expect this to be the
                             one theorem stated-but-not-transliterated, like 9.1's =~.
  Third Isomorphism          Judson gives NO PROOF (left to the reader). Needs two
                             nested quotients, G/N and (G/N)/(H/N).

CAVEAT from mod-n's own header, worth knowing before building G/N over a cut sort: the
quotient template works over a STANDALONE substrate but composing it over a cut-sort
substrate hits two model-system walls. Building G/N over a bare abstract Grp with
inSubgroup as a plain predicate guard — which is what the probe does — is fine.

